<section class="mss-faq" id="faq">
  <div class="mss-faq-inner">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">What is a music split sheet?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">A music split sheet is a legally binding agreement between songwriters, producers, and other collaborators that documents each party's ownership percentage of a song. It covers both the composition (publishing splits) and the master recording (master splits).</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">How does the process work?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">Buy the PDF ($7, or $12 for the LOD bundle) via secure Shopify checkout. Your download starts immediately after payment and a download link is also sent to your email. Fill in the fields on your computer or print it and fill by hand.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">Can I reuse it for multiple songs?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">Yes &mdash; that's the point. You buy it once and it's yours forever. Use it for every song, every collab, every release. No subscriptions, no per-song fees.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">Do I need a split sheet for every song?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">Yes &mdash; any song with more than one contributor should have a split sheet. Without one, ownership disputes can arise when a song is synced, streamed, or sampled.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">What&rsquo;s the difference between publishing splits and master recording splits?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">Publishing splits cover the composition &mdash; the melody and lyrics &mdash; and determine how songwriting royalties are divided. Master recording splits cover the actual recorded version and determine how master royalties are divided among producers, artists, and labels.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">Is this a legal document?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">Yes. A signed split sheet is a binding agreement between all parties. It documents the agreed ownership percentages for both the composition and the master recording. We recommend all parties retain a signed copy.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">What format do I receive?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">A fillable PDF &mdash; type directly into every field, or print and fill by hand. It can be signed digitally using Adobe Acrobat, DocuSign, or any PDF signing tool, or printed and signed in the room.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">What is a Letter of Direction (LOD)?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">A Letter of Direction tells SoundExchange (or another neighboring rights society) how to split and direct digital performance royalties. If you have a producer, featured artist, or label who needs royalties routed separately, the LOD is required.</p></div>
    <div class="mss-faq-item"><p class="mss-faq-q">Does this work outside the United States?</p><p class="mss-faq-a">Yes. The PRO fields support international societies including PRS (UK), SOCAN (Canada), APRA AMCOS (Australia/New Zealand), ASCAP, BMI, SESAC, and others.</p></div>
  </div>
</section>

